{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01F86298KDgC8shCQ5eUZcHJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Public ABI (from `nm -D` on the C shared object) — exactly one symbol:\n4\t//!   * `tfm`\n5\t//!\n6\t//! The header `c_src/include/lib.h` declares no namespace/renaming macros, so\n7\t//! the linker symbol is the plain source-level name `tfm`.\n8\t//!\n9\t//! # Bit-exactness\n10\t//!\n11\t//! The C source is reproduced literally, including the operand ordering of\n12\t//! every floating point expression, so that results are bit-for-bit identical.\n13\t//!\n14\t//! One subtlety required care. A naive transliteration compiles to the same\n15\t//! *arithmetic* as GCC, but LLVM freely **commutes** the operands of `addss` /\n16\t//! `mulss` (and auto-vectorizes `mulps`), because FP add/mul are commutative\n17\t//! for ordinary values. They are *not* commutative in NaN payload propagation:\n18\t//! x86 SSE returns the quieted **destination** operand if it is NaN, and only\n19\t//! otherwise the source operand. So `(-nan) + (+nan)` yields `-nan` or `+nan`\n20\t//! depending purely on which operand the compiler put in the destination\n21\t//! register. That made expressions mixing several NaNs (reachable from inputs\n22\t//! such as `(0.0, -inf, NaN)`, where `2.0f*dx2*dy2` is `-inf*0.0` and produces\n23\t//! the x86 \"QNaN indefinite\" `0xFFC00000`) differ from GCC's output.\n24\t//!\n25\t//! The helpers below therefore compute the operation normally — which is\n26\t//! bit-exact whenever the result is not NaN — and, only on the rare NaN\n27\t//! outcome, impose x86 SSE's deterministic NaN selection rules. Because every\n28\t//! NaN outcome is decided explicitly, the optimizer is free to reorder or\n29\t//! vectorize the underlying arithmetic without changing the result, and the\n30\t//! operand order that GCC actually chose is then reproduced explicitly at each\n31\t//! call site.\n32\t//!\n33\t//! Worth recording: **the C library is not self-consistent here.** GCC picks\n34\t//! different operand orders at different optimization levels, so `-O0`,\n35\t//! `-O1`/`-O2`/`-Os` and `-O3` builds of `c_src` disagree with *each other* on\n36\t//! these NaN payloads. The divergence needs a NaN *input*: with finite inputs\n37\t//! every NaN that can arise is the canonical indefinite `0xFFC00000`, so\n38\t//! payload selection cannot matter and all builds agree (verified over 18M\n39\t//! random finite inputs). This translation matches the build that `c_src`'s own\n40\t//! `CMakeLists.txt` produces — `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` is unset, so no `-O` flag is\n41\t//! passed, i.e. `-O0`, which is also what a plain `gcc -shared` glob gives.\n42\t//!\n43\t//! Original C:\n44\t//! ```c\n45\t//! void tfm(float *dest, const float *src, int count) {\n46\t//!     int i;\n47\t//!     for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n48\t//!         if (src[0] < src[1]) {\n49\t//!             float dx2 = src[0];\n50\t//!             float dy2 = src[1];\n51\t//!             float dxy = src[2];\n52\t//!             float sqd = (dy2 * dy2) - (2.0f * dx2 * dy2) + (dx2 * dx2) +\n53\t//!                         (4.0f * dxy * dxy);\n54\t//!             float lambda =\n55\t//!                 0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf((((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd))));\n56\t//!             dest[0] = dx2 - lambda;\n57\t//!             dest[1] = dxy;\n58\t//!         } else {\n59\t//!             float dy2 = src[0];\n60\t//!             float dx2 = src[1];\n61\t//!             float dxy = src[2];\n62\t//!             float sqd = (dy2 * dy2) - (2.0f * dx2 * dy2) + (dx2 * dx2) +\n63\t//!                         (4.0f * dxy * dxy);\n64\t//!             float lambda =\n65\t//!                 0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf((((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd))));\n66\t//!             dest[0] = dxy;\n67\t//!             dest[1] = dx2 - lambda;\n68\t//!         }\n69\t//!         src += 3;\n70\t//!         dest += 2;\n71\t//!     }\n72\t//! }\n73\t//! ```\n74\t\n75\tuse core::ffi::c_int;\n76\t\n77\t/// x86 \"QNaN floating-point indefinite\": the NaN produced by SSE when an\n78\t/// operation is invalid but neither operand was already a NaN (`inf - inf`,\n79\t/// `0 * inf`, `sqrt` of a negative). Note the set sign bit.\n80\tconst INDEFINITE: u32 = 0xFFC0_0000;\n81\t\n82\t/// Quiet a NaN the way SSE does when it propagates an operand: set the\n83\t/// mantissa MSB, leaving the sign bit and the rest of the payload alone.\n84\t#[inline(always)]\n85\tfn quiet(x: f32) -> f32 {\n86\t    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n87\t}\n88\t\n89\t/// Resolve the NaN result of a binary SSE op with `a` as the destination\n90\t/// operand and `b` as the source operand.\n91\t///\n92\t/// Kept out of line and `cold`: NaN results are the rare case, so the hot path\n93\t/// stays a bare FP instruction plus a NaN test.\n94\t#[cold]\n95\t#[inline(never)]\n96\tfn nan_binop(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n97\t    if a.is_nan() {\n98\t        // Destination operand is NaN: it wins, quieted.\n99\t        quiet(a)\n100\t    } else if b.is_nan() {\n101\t        // Otherwise the source operand, quieted.\n102\t        quiet(b)\n103\t    } else {\n104\t        // Neither operand was NaN, so the op itself was invalid.\n105\t        f32::from_bits(INDEFINITE)\n106\t    }\n107\t}\n108\t\n109\t/// `a + b` with x86 SSE `ADDSS a, b` NaN semantics (`a` = destination).\n110\t#[inline(always)]\n111\tfn fadd(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n112\t    let r = a + b;\n113\t    if r.is_nan() {\n114\t        nan_binop(a, b)\n115\t    } else {\n116\t        r\n117\t    }\n118\t}\n119\t\n120\t/// `a - b` with x86 SSE `SUBSS a, b` NaN semantics (`a` = destination).\n121\t#[inline(always)]\n122\tfn fsub(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n123\t    let r = a - b;\n124\t    if r.is_nan() {\n125\t        nan_binop(a, b)\n126\t    } else {\n127\t        r\n128\t    }\n129\t}\n130\t\n131\t/// `a * b` with x86 SSE `MULSS a, b` NaN semantics (`a` = destination).\n132\t#[inline(always)]\n133\tfn fmul(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n134\t    let r = a * b;\n135\t    if r.is_nan() {\n136\t        nan_binop(a, b)\n137\t    } else {\n138\t        r\n139\t    }\n140\t}\n141\t\n142\t/// `sqrtf(x)`.\n143\t///\n144\t/// The reference build calls glibc's `sqrtf@plt`. Measured against glibc, a NaN\n145\t/// argument comes back as exactly `quiet(x)` (sign and payload preserved,\n146\t/// mantissa MSB forced on), which is what `SQRTSS` does too.\n147\t///\n148\t/// The negative-argument case is unreachable: the preceding `comiss`/`jbe`\n149\t/// already replaced any negative `sqd` with the `0.0f` constant from `.rodata`\n150\t/// before the call, so `errno` is never set. It is handled here for\n151\t/// completeness, using SSE's QNaN indefinite.\n152\t#[inline(always)]\n153\tfn fsqrt(x: f32) -> f32 {\n154\t    if x.is_nan() {\n155\t        return quiet(x);\n156\t    }\n157\t    if x < 0.0f32 {\n158\t        // `sqrtss` of a negative operand yields QNaN indefinite. (`-0.0f` is\n159\t        // not negative by this test and correctly returns `-0.0f` below.)\n160\t        return f32::from_bits(INDEFINITE);\n161\t    }\n162\t    x.sqrt()\n163\t}\n164\t\n165\t/// Faithful translation of the C macro expansion\n166\t/// `(((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd))`.\n167\t///\n168\t/// In C the integer literal `0` is converted to `float` by the usual arithmetic\n169\t/// conversions on both the relational and the conditional operator, so the\n170\t/// whole expression has type `float`. This is deliberately *not* `f32::max` /\n171\t/// `fmaxf`:\n172\t///   * when `sqd` is NaN the comparison `0 > sqd` is false, so the NaN is\n173\t///     returned and `sqrtf` then propagates it;\n174\t///   * when `sqd` is `-0.0f` the comparison is false as well, so `-0.0f` is\n175\t///     returned and `sqrtf(-0.0f)` yields `-0.0f`.\n176\t///\n177\t/// (LLVM lowers this to `maxss`, which happens to have exactly these\n178\t/// semantics, so the transformation is sound.)\n179\t#[inline(always)]\n180\tfn clamp_nonneg_c(sqd: f32) -> f32 {\n181\t    if 0.0f32 > sqd {\n182\t        0.0f32\n183\t    } else {\n184\t        sqd\n185\t    }\n186\t}\n187\t\n188\t/// The shared body of the two branches, kept as a single helper so the operand\n189\t/// order of the arithmetic is written exactly once.\n190\t///\n191\t/// Computes\n192\t/// ```text\n193\t/// sqd    = (dy2*dy2) - (2.0f*dx2*dy2) + (dx2*dx2) + (4.0f*dxy*dxy)\n194\t/// lambda = 0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf(max(0, sqd)))\n195\t/// ```\n196\t/// with C's left-to-right associativity:\n197\t/// `((((dy2*dy2) - ((2.0f*dx2)*dy2)) + (dx2*dx2)) + ((4.0f*dxy)*dxy))`.\n198\t///\n199\t/// Note the two C branches bind `dx2`/`dy2` to *opposite* inputs, so they are\n200\t/// genuinely different rounding sequences; passing the arguments in the right\n201\t/// order at each call site is what reproduces that.\n202\t#[inline(always)]\n203\tfn lambda_of(dx2: f32, dy2: f32, dxy: f32) -> f32 {\n204\t    // Accumulator for `(dy2*dy2) - (2.0f*dx2*dy2) + (dx2*dx2)`, in source order.\n205\t    // `2.0f * dx2` is emitted by GCC as `ADDSS dst=dx2, src=dx2`, which agrees\n206\t    // with `fmul(2.0, dx2)` on NaN propagation (only `dx2` can be the NaN) and\n207\t    // is exact for every finite value, so the plain multiply is kept.\n208\t    let acc: f32 = fadd(\n209\t        fsub(fmul(dy2, dy2), fmul(fmul(2.0f32, dx2), dy2)),\n210\t        fmul(dx2, dx2),\n211\t    );\n212\t\n213\t    // `4.0f * dxy * dxy`, emitted as `MULSS dst=4.0f, src=dxy` then\n214\t    // `MULSS dst=(4.0f*dxy), src=dxy`.\n215\t    let dxy_term: f32 = fmul(fmul(4.0f32, dxy), dxy);\n216\t\n217\t    // Source order is `acc + (4.0f*dxy*dxy)`, but GCC commutes this add and\n218\t    // emits `ADDSS dst=(4.0f*dxy*dxy), src=acc` (`tfm+0xac` / `tfm+0x194` in\n219\t    // the reference object). The choice is observable: when `dxy` is a NaN and\n220\t    // `acc` is also NaN -- e.g. `dy2*dy2` and `dx2*dx2` both overflow to `inf`\n221\t    // so that `inf - inf` produces the indefinite `0xFFC00000` -- the\n222\t    // destination operand's payload is the one that survives. `dxy`'s NaN must\n223\t    // therefore be the destination.\n224\t    let sqd: f32 = fadd(dxy_term, acc);\n225\t\n226\t    // `0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf(...))`, all in source order here:\n227\t    //   `ADDSS dst=dy2,        src=dx2`\n228\t    //   `ADDSS dst=(dy2+dx2),  src=sqrtf(...)`\n229\t    //   `MULSS dst=0.5f,       src=(...)`\n230\t    // The sum is the destination of the outer add, so its payload wins over the\n231\t    // square root's. (The `* 0.5f` order is immaterial: only one of `0.5f` and\n232\t    // the sum can ever be NaN.)\n233\t    fmul(0.5f32, fadd(fadd(dy2, dx2), fsqrt(clamp_nonneg_c(sqd))))\n234\t}\n235\t\n236\t/// `void tfm(float *dest, const float *src, int count);`\n237\t///\n238\t/// Consumes `count` triples of floats from `src` and writes `count` pairs of\n239\t/// floats to `dest`.\n240\t///\n241\t/// # Safety\n242\t///\n243\t/// Same contract as the C function: `src` must be valid for reading\n244\t/// `3 * count` floats and `dest` must be valid for writing `2 * count` floats\n245\t/// whenever `count > 0`. Non-positive `count` performs no accesses at all,\n246\t/// exactly as the C `for` loop does.\n247\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n248\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn tfm(dest: *mut f32, src: *const f32, count: c_int) {\n249\t    // Local mutable copies of the pointer parameters; C advances the\n250\t    // parameters themselves at the bottom of each iteration.\n251\t    let mut dest: *mut f32 = dest;\n252\t    let mut src: *const f32 = src;\n253\t\n254\t    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n255\t    while i < count {\n256\t        let s0: f32 = unsafe { *src.add(0) };\n257\t        let s1: f32 = unsafe { *src.add(1) };\n258\t        let s2: f32 = unsafe { *src.add(2) };\n259\t\n260\t        // NaN in either operand makes this false, selecting the `else` branch,\n261\t        // exactly as C's `<` does.\n262\t        if s0 < s1 {\n263\t            let dx2: f32 = s0;\n264\t            let dy2: f32 = s1;\n265\t            let dxy: f32 = s2;\n266\t\n267\t            let lambda: f32 = lambda_of(dx2, dy2, dxy);\n268\t\n269\t            unsafe {\n270\t                *dest.add(0) = fsub(dx2, lambda);\n271\t                *dest.add(1) = dxy;\n272\t            }\n273\t        } else {\n274\t            let dy2: f32 = s0;\n275\t            let dx2: f32 = s1;\n276\t            let dxy: f32 = s2;\n277\t\n278\t            let lambda: f32 = lambda_of(dx2, dy2, dxy);\n279\t\n280\t            unsafe {\n281\t                *dest.add(0) = dxy;\n282\t                *dest.add(1) = fsub(dx2, lambda);\n283\t            }\n284\t        }\n285\t\n286\t        src = unsafe { src.add(3) };\n287\t        dest = unsafe { dest.add(2) };\n288\t        i = i.wrapping_add(1);\n289\t    }\n290\t}\n291\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2d9b716-85f9-4088-b8c7-7e0e7bf70850","uuid":"d6bd59dd-13a5-4ace-8e55-a778f3995ba2","timestamp":"2026-09-05T02:11:09.235Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-HnrXsH/translation/src/lib.rs","content":"//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n//!\n//! Public ABI (from `nm -D` on the C shared object) — exactly one symbol:\n//!   * `tfm`\n//!\n//! The header `c_src/include/lib.h` declares no namespace/renaming macros, so\n//! the linker symbol is the plain source-level name `tfm`.\n//!\n//! # Bit-exactness\n//!\n//! The C source is reproduced literally, including the operand ordering of\n//! every floating point expression, so that results are bit-for-bit identical.\n//!\n//! One subtlety required care. A naive transliteration compiles to the same\n//! *arithmetic* as GCC, but LLVM freely **commutes** the operands of `addss` /\n//! `mulss` (and auto-vectorizes `mulps`), because FP add/mul are commutative\n//! for ordinary values. They are *not* commutative in NaN payload propagation:\n//! x86 SSE returns the quieted **destination** operand if it is NaN, and only\n//! otherwise the source operand. So `(-nan) + (+nan)` yields `-nan` or `+nan`\n//! depending purely on which operand the compiler put in the destination\n//! register. That made expressions mixing several NaNs (reachable from inputs\n//! such as `(0.0, -inf, NaN)`, where `2.0f*dx2*dy2` is `-inf*0.0` and produces\n//! the x86 \"QNaN indefinite\" `0xFFC00000`) differ from GCC's output.\n//!\n//! The helpers below therefore compute the operation normally — which is\n//! bit-exact whenever the result is not NaN — and, only on the rare NaN\n//! outcome, impose x86 SSE's deterministic NaN selection rules. Because every\n//! NaN outcome is decided explicitly, the optimizer is free to reorder or\n//! vectorize the underlying arithmetic without changing the result, and the\n//! operand order that GCC actually chose is then reproduced explicitly at each\n//! call site.\n//!\n//! Worth recording: **the C library is not self-consistent here.** GCC picks\n//! different operand orders at different optimization levels, so `-O0`,\n//! `-O1`/`-O2`/`-Os` and `-O3` builds of `c_src` disagree with *each other* on\n//! these NaN payloads. The divergence needs a NaN *input*: with finite inputs\n//! every NaN that can arise is the canonical indefinite `0xFFC00000`, so\n//! payload selection cannot matter and all builds agree (verified over 18M\n//! random finite inputs). This translation matches the build that `c_src`'s own\n//! `CMakeLists.txt` produces — `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` is unset, so no `-O` flag is\n//! passed, i.e. `-O0`, which is also what a plain `gcc -shared` glob gives.\n//!\n//! Original C:\n//! ```c\n//! void tfm(float *dest, const float *src, int count) {\n//!     int i;\n//!     for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n//!         if (src[0] < src[1]) {\n//!             float dx2 = src[0];\n//!             float dy2 = src[1];\n//!             float dxy = src[2];\n//!             float sqd = (dy2 * dy2) - (2.0f * dx2 * dy2) + (dx2 * dx2) +\n//!                         (4.0f * dxy * dxy);\n//!             float lambda =\n//!                 0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf((((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd))));\n//!             dest[0] = dx2 - lambda;\n//!             dest[1] = dxy;\n//!         } else {\n//!             float dy2 = src[0];\n//!             float dx2 = src[1];\n//!             float dxy = src[2];\n//!             float sqd = (dy2 * dy2) - (2.0f * dx2 * dy2) + (dx2 * dx2) +\n//!                         (4.0f * dxy * dxy);\n//!             float lambda =\n//!                 0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf((((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd))));\n//!             dest[0] = dxy;\n//!             dest[1] = dx2 - lambda;\n//!         }\n//!         src += 3;\n//!         dest += 2;\n//!     }\n//! }\n//! ```\n\nuse core::ffi::c_int;\n\n/// x86 \"QNaN floating-point indefinite\": the NaN produced by SSE when an\n/// operation is invalid but neither operand was already a NaN (`inf - inf`,\n/// `0 * inf`, `sqrt` of a negative). Note the set sign bit.\nconst INDEFINITE: u32 = 0xFFC0_0000;\n\n/// Quiet a NaN the way SSE does when it propagates an operand: set the\n/// mantissa MSB, leaving the sign bit and the rest of the payload alone.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn quiet(x: f32) -> f32 {\n    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n}\n\n/// Resolve the NaN result of a binary SSE op with `a` as the destination\n/// operand and `b` as the source operand.\n///\n/// Kept out of line and `cold`: NaN results are the rare case, so the hot path\n/// stays a bare FP instruction plus a NaN test.\n#[cold]\n#[inline(never)]\nfn nan_binop(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    if a.is_nan() {\n        // Destination operand is NaN: it wins, quieted.\n        quiet(a)\n    } else if b.is_nan() {\n        // Otherwise the source operand, quieted.\n        quiet(b)\n    } else {\n        // Neither operand was NaN, so the op itself was invalid.\n        f32::from_bits(INDEFINITE)\n    }\n}\n\n/// `a + b` with x86 SSE `ADDSS a, b` NaN semantics (`a` = destination).\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fadd(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    let r = a + b;\n    if r.is_nan() {\n        nan_binop(a, b)\n    } else {\n        r\n    }\n}\n\n/// `a - b` with x86 SSE `SUBSS a, b` NaN semantics (`a` = destination).\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fsub(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    let r = a - b;\n    if r.is_nan() {\n        nan_binop(a, b)\n    } else {\n        r\n    }\n}\n\n/// `a * b` with x86 SSE `MULSS a, b` NaN semantics (`a` = destination).\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fmul(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    let r = a * b;\n    if r.is_nan() {\n        nan_binop(a, b)\n    } else {\n        r\n    }\n}\n\n/// `sqrtf(x)`.\n///\n/// The reference build calls glibc's `sqrtf@plt`. Measured against glibc, a NaN\n/// argument comes back as exactly `quiet(x)` (sign and payload preserved,\n/// mantissa MSB forced on), which is what `SQRTSS` does too.\n///\n/// The negative-argument case is unreachable: the preceding `comiss`/`jbe`\n/// already replaced any negative `sqd` with the `0.0f` constant from `.rodata`\n/// before the call, so `errno` is never set. It is handled here for\n/// completeness, using SSE's QNaN indefinite.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fsqrt(x: f32) -> f32 {\n    if x.is_nan() {\n        return quiet(x);\n    }\n    if x < 0.0f32 {\n        // `sqrtss` of a negative operand yields QNaN indefinite. (`-0.0f` is\n        // not negative by this test and correctly returns `-0.0f` below.)\n        return f32::from_bits(INDEFINITE);\n    }\n    x.sqrt()\n}\n\n/// Faithful translation of the C macro expansion\n/// `(((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd))`.\n///\n/// In C the integer literal `0` is converted to `float` by the usual arithmetic\n/// conversions on both the relational and the conditional operator, so the\n/// whole expression has type `float`. This is deliberately *not* `f32::max` /\n/// `fmaxf`:\n///   * when `sqd` is NaN the comparison `0 > sqd` is false, so the NaN is\n///     returned and `sqrtf` then propagates it;\n///   * when `sqd` is `-0.0f` the comparison is false as well, so `-0.0f` is\n///     returned and `sqrtf(-0.0f)` yields `-0.0f`.\n///\n/// (LLVM lowers this to `maxss`, which happens to have exactly these\n/// semantics, so the transformation is sound.)\n#[inline(always)]\nfn clamp_nonneg_c(sqd: f32) -> f32 {\n    if 0.0f32 > sqd {\n        0.0f32\n    } else {\n        sqd\n    }\n}\n\n/// The shared body of the two branches, kept as a single helper so the operand\n/// order of the arithmetic is written exactly once.\n///\n/// Computes\n/// ```text\n/// sqd    = (dy2*dy2) - (2.0f*dx2*dy2) + (dx2*dx2) + (4.0f*dxy*dxy)\n/// lambda = 0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf(max(0, sqd)))\n/// ```\n/// with C's left-to-right associativity:\n/// `((((dy2*dy2) - ((2.0f*dx2)*dy2)) + (dx2*dx2)) + ((4.0f*dxy)*dxy))`.\n///\n/// Note the two C branches bind `dx2`/`dy2` to *opposite* inputs, so they are\n/// genuinely different rounding sequences; passing the arguments in the right\n/// order at each call site is what reproduces that.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn lambda_of(dx2: f32, dy2: f32, dxy: f32) -> f32 {\n    // Accumulator for `(dy2*dy2) - (2.0f*dx2*dy2) + (dx2*dx2)`, in source order.\n    // `2.0f * dx2` is emitted by GCC as `ADDSS dst=dx2, src=dx2`, which agrees\n    // with `fmul(2.0, dx2)` on NaN propagation (only `dx2` can be the NaN) and\n    // is exact for every finite value, so the plain multiply is kept.\n    let acc: f32 = fadd(\n        fsub(fmul(dy2, dy2), fmul(fmul(2.0f32, dx2), dy2)),\n        fmul(dx2, dx2),\n    );\n\n    // `4.0f * dxy * dxy`, emitted as `MULSS dst=4.0f, src=dxy` then\n    // `MULSS dst=(4.0f*dxy), src=dxy`.\n    let dxy_term: f32 = fmul(fmul(4.0f32, dxy), dxy);\n\n    // Source order is `acc + (4.0f*dxy*dxy)`, but GCC commutes this add and\n    // emits `ADDSS dst=(4.0f*dxy*dxy), src=acc` (`tfm+0xac` / `tfm+0x194` in\n    // the reference object). The choice is observable: when `dxy` is a NaN and\n    // `acc` is also NaN -- e.g. `dy2*dy2` and `dx2*dx2` both overflow to `inf